The early return fires unconditionally whenever the file looks transformed and has no named exports, even when the framework import is missing or incomplete. The rest of the function (lines ~229–264) is the only code that handles adding/patching the `defineMain`/`definePreview` import specifier. Two existing test cases directly exercise this path — 'should add missing import when already transformed but import is absent' and 'should add missing specifier when already transformed but defineMain is not in existing import' — and both would regress under this change because `info.source` is returned before the import-fixup logic runs.

Fix: gate the early return on a `hasCorrectImport` check, the same way the surrounding code does for the broader `shouldSkipTransform` condition:
```ts
const hasCorrectImport = programNode.body.some(
  (node) =>
    t.isImportDeclaration(node) &&
    node.importKind !== 'type' &&
    node.source.value === expectedImportSource &&
    node.specifiers.some(
      (spec) =>
        t.isImportSpecifier(spec) &&
        t.isIdentifier(spec.imported) &&
        spec.imported.name === methodName
    )
);
if (isAlreadyTransformed && !hasNamedExports && hasCorrectImport) {
  return info.source;
}
```

`t.isCallExpression(node.declaration)` checks the raw AST node directly without unwrapping TypeScript type-annotation syntax. In TypeScript files, the default export declaration is often wrapped in a `TSSatisfiesExpression` (`export default defineMain({}) satisfies StorybookConfig`) or `TSAsExpression` (`export default defineMain({}) as StorybookConfig`). In those cases `node.declaration` is *not* a `CallExpression`, so `isAlreadyTransformed` evaluates to `false` and the early return never fires — the file gets fed through `recast` and still suffers the Windows CRLF mutation.

The existing code in Scenario 2 already handles this with `config._unwrap(node.declaration)`. Use the same unwrapping here:
```ts
const isAlreadyTransformed = programNode.body.some((node) => {
  if (!t.isExportDefaultDeclaration(node)) return false;
  const declaration = config._unwrap(node.declaration as t.Node);
  return (
    t.isCallExpression(declaration) &&
    t.isIdentifier(declaration.callee) &&
    declaration.callee.name === methodName
  );
});
```
